From f6b1b24fbbcdabb5b47412339ec3c6e6a2aeac60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 09:53:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Port the MobileNet backbone to eager TensorFlow 2 / Keras

backbones/mobilenet.py, backbones/mobilenet_v2.py and nnets/conv_blocks.py
are a vendored copy of the tf_slim model-zoo MobilenetV2 -- 1141 lines
carrying 57 slim.* and 11 tf.compat.v1 references before 0014.  They use
slim only for its layer wrappers (conv2d, separable_conv2d, batch_norm,
arg_scope), all of which have exact Keras equivalents, so the whole
backbone fits in one eager tf.keras.Model.

backbones/mobilenet_v2_keras.py is that model.  MobilenetV2Keras registers
with KerasBackboneFactory under "mobilenet"; PoseMobileNetKeras derives
from BaseKerasPoseNet and registers with PoseNetFactory under
engine=KERAS_ENGINE, so resolve_engine picks it up from pose_cfg.yaml's
tf_engine key or DLC_TF_ENGINE.  The tf_slim path is untouched and stays
the default.  Every layer carries the tf_slim variable scope it replaces
and implements slim_weights(), so slim_weight_map(), load_tf1_checkpoint
and save_tf1_checkpoint work with no MobileNet-specific plumbing: 266
weights mapped, unmapped_weights() empty.  The heads are
keras_prediction_layer via BaseKerasPoseNet._add_head, at the same
pose/{part_pred,locref_pred,pairwise_pred,intermediate_supervision}/block4
scopes.

Since 0020 made core/predict dispatch on the engine, this is the piece
that makes a net_type=mobilenet_v2_* project actually runnable on the
eager path: setup_pose_prediction now builds the Keras net, restores a
snapshot through load_tf1_checkpoint and returns score maps that go
straight into extract_cnn_output and argmax_pose_predict.

The two things that needed care

Inference-time batch norm, i.e. patch 0014.  PoseMobileNet builds the
backbone through mobilenet_v2.training_scope(), which defaults to
is_training=True, so all 52 batch-norm layers normalise with batch
statistics even at inference.  0014 keeps that -- switching to the stored
moving averages costs 4.72 -> 12.79 px test error -- and removes only the
dependence on batch composition, by building the backbone one image at a
time and concatenating.  SlimBatchNorm expresses the same thing directly:
it reduces over [height, width] instead of [batch, height, width].  That
is one pass instead of N, it drops 0014's 3.5 s -> 25.7 s graph
construction, and it works for a dynamic batch dimension, which the
replication trick could not: a tf.function traced on [None, H, W, 3] --
the shape export_model uses -- gives the same prediction for an image at
batch sizes 1, 2, 4 and 8 and under shuffled companions, to 2.4e-6 in the
part_pred logits.  Exported MobileNet graphs consumed in batches were the
one case 0014 left unfixed.

Unlike the resnet backbone, which PoseResnet builds with is_training=False
unconditionally so its batch norm never updates, this one really does
train: dumping the tf_slim graph shows 52 FusedBatchNormV3 nodes with
is_training=True and 104 UPDATE_OPS in the training graph.
PoseMobileNetKeras.extract_features therefore passes is_training through
to the backbone, and there is a test that the moving averages move when,
and only when, is_training is set.

The caller's pose_cfg.  MobilenetV2Keras keeps no reference to cfg at all
-- only scalars and a fresh copy of mean_pixel -- so tf.keras.Model's
attribute tracking never touches the caller's dict; PoseMobileNetKeras
gets cfg through BaseKerasPoseNet, which uses set_untracked.  Verified the
way 0019 asks: write_plainconfig, build the net, write_plainconfig again,
byte-identical with no !!python tags.  Reverting either half makes six of
the new tests fail, and write_plainconfig then raises RepresenterError on
ListWrapper([123.68, 116.779, 103.939]) rather than corrupting silently.

Equivalence against the patched tf_slim implementation

Over all four stock net_types at batch sizes 1 and 4, 73 tensors each (19
layer_N endpoints, 50 intermediate endpoints, the feature map and the
part_pred / locref / part_pred_interm heads), 128x160 input, with the real
ImageNet weights restored:

  every variable maps 1:1, 266 of 266, no unmapped Keras weight
  end_point key sets identical, 69 of 69
  worst relative deviation   8.1e-5, always on layer_19 (relu6-saturated)
  keypoints through predict.argmax_pose_predict
                             max 5.9e-4 px, mean 2.8e-4 px
  peak likelihoods           max 4.4e-6

With TF_ENABLE_ONEDNN_OPTS=0 on both sides, which removes the difference
between oneDNN's graph-mode fusions and eager execution, those become
6.8e-5, 4.0e-4 px and 2.1e-4 px.

That residual is the float32 noise floor of this network, not a defect of
the port.  Re-associating one line of the same Keras code -- (x-m)*s+b
instead of x*s+(b-m*s), algebraically identical and both matching
fused_batch_norm to 2 ulp in isolation -- moves the feature map by 2.3e-4,
and running the very same model in float64 moves it by 2.2e-4, against
3.7e-4 for the gap to tf_slim; evaluating the port in float64 brings it
closer to tf_slim's float32 result (2.7e-4), not further away.
MobileNet's 52 chained batch-norm layers amplify a per-layer rounding
difference the same way 0014 measured them amplifying a real
perturbation: 18.7% at layer 1 becoming 72% at layer 52.  Two correct
float32 implementations of this network cannot agree more closely than
this, and the port sits inside that floor.

Batch independence holds exactly where batch norm decides it.  Across all
four multipliers the backbone feature map at batch 4 is bit-identical to
the batch-1 one, 0.000e+00, however the batch is composed.  The heads are
not quite: Conv2DTranspose accumulates differently for different batch
sizes, which moves the keypoints by 1e-5 px and is the same last-bit noise
the tf_slim graph shows between its own batch-1 and batch-4 builds
(1.2e-6).  End to end through core/predict.setup_pose_prediction the same
frame gives the same keypoints at batch size 1 and 4 to 2.0e-5 px.  As a
control, SlimBatchNorm also offers the reduction the unpatched code
performs (bn_mode="batch"), which reproduces stock tf_slim to 2.9e-5 -
5.5e-4 and exhibits the bug 0014 describes: at batch size 4 the keypoints
of a fixed image move by 51 to 130 px depending on its companions.

The checkpoint bridge works in both directions.  The ImageNet checkpoint
loads with 266 of 266 tensors bit-identical and nothing left over; five
tf.GradientTape steps train the net, 1.97901 -> 1.86072, moving 258 of the
266 tensors (the eight that do not are project/BatchNorm/beta on residual
blocks, whose gradients underflow float32 at this learning rate -- no
trainable variable got a None gradient); save_tf1_checkpoint writes it
back out, and a bare tf.compat.v1.train.Saver().restore reads it into the
unmodified tf_slim graph and reproduces the feature map to 4.9e-5
relative.

Landmines checked rather than assumed.  slim.l2_regularizer(s) is
s*sum(w^2)/2 while keras.regularizers.l2(s) is s*sum(w^2), so the
convolutions go through layers.slim_l2_regularizer; the 35 backbone
convolutions and 3 heads produce 38 regularization losses and the
depthwise kernels produce none, matching training_scope's
weights_regularizer=None for separable_conv2d.  Nothing sums model.losses
into the loss, because slim.learning.create_train_op never added them.
slim's separable_conv2d with num_outputs=None is depthwise only, with no
pointwise stage, so it maps to DepthwiseConv2D and not SeparableConv2D;
depth_multiplier=1 gives the same [k, k, in, 1] kernel slim stores as
depthwise_weights, under that name and not "weights".  Batch norm is
pinned to slim.batch_norm's epsilon of 1e-3, to training_scope's decay of
0.997 and to V2_DEF's scale=True rather than left on the Keras defaults or
on layers.py's SLIM_BN_* constants, which are slim's unmodified ones.
The 15th op's stride is 1, not the 2 of the upstream architecture, which
is DeepLabCut's change to get output stride 16.

Tests

tests/test_nnets_keras_mobilenet.py, 39 eager-only tests covering the
contract, the end_point names, the checkpoint round trip, the three
batch-norm modes, the dynamic-batch graph, the pose_cfg guarantee and the
core/predict entry point.  Its eager fixture leaves eager on, like the
other Keras modules, so the four compose in any collection order; that was
checked over seven different orders.

Two config tests are also generalised, and this fixes three failures that
predate this change.  Both asserted that building a Keras net leaves the
caller's pose_cfg byte-identical, which is not the property anyone wants:
PoseMultiNet turns cfg["multi_stage"] off for a backbone that is not a
resnet, and PoseEfficientNet.__init__ has always filled in
cfg["use_batch_norm"] and cfg["use_drop_out"], both of them in the tf_slim
engine as much as the Keras one, and callers rely on the write-through --
0022 removed a config copy that was silently swallowing exactly this.  The
property that matters is parity between the engines: build the tf_slim net
on a second copy of the config and compare against what it leaves behind.
That needs no enumeration of which net writes which key, so a backbone
added later is covered without editing the test again.  What the tests
still catch unchanged is the corruption 0019 fixed -- a ListWrapper in the
caller's dict, or a !!python tag in the YAML -- and they still have teeth:
making PoseMobileNetKeras write one spurious key and leak one list fails
seven of them.

  before: 10 failed, 198 passed, 4 skipped
   after:  7 failed, 244 passed, 0 skipped

The three that go green are
test_nnets_efficientnet_keras.py::test_pose_net_leaves_the_caller_config_alone
and test_no_registered_keras_net_touches_the_callers_config[{4,8}-efficientnet-b0].
The seven that remain are FileNotFoundError on test data the sdist does
not ship, identical before and after, as are the 91 collection errors.
All four Keras suites together: 132 passed, nothing skipped.

Reference counts are unchanged in the shipped package: 111 tf.compat.v1
and 96 slim. occurrences in deeplabcut/ before and after, because the
tf_slim MobileNet has to stay selectable.  The new backbone contains
neither.  What retiring the tf_slim MobileNet would remove is 10
tf.compat.v1 and 42 slim. occurrences -- backbones/mobilenet.py (6 and
25), backbones/mobilenet_v2.py (0 and 16) and the tf_slim half of
nnets/mobilenet.py (4 and 1) -- plus nnets/conv_blocks.py's 4 and 7, which
can only go once PoseMultiNet stops importing expanded_conv from it.

Not covered.  PoseMultiNetKeras will now find a mobilenet backbone, since
it asks KerasBackboneFactory for one, but it indexes end_points by resnet
block names and would fail at the first forward pass; that mirrors the
tf_slim net, whose own mobilenet branch is unreachable because it needs
stride < 8 and nothing writes such a value.
